# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Deep content data for the 91-day New Testament plan.
# All commentary is paraphrased/summarized in original wording -- no verbatim
# quotation of copyrighted authors, and Scripture is referenced, not
# reproduced at length, out of respect for NLT copyright.

BOOK_INTROS = [
    ("Matthew", "The King and His Kingdom",
     "Author & Date: Matthew (Levi), a former tax collector and one of the Twelve, writing likely between the 60s-80s AD.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Jewish believers wrestling with how faith in Jesus fits their Jewish heritage, possibly facing pressure from local synagogues.",
     "Historical Setting: Judea was under direct Roman rule through prefects/procurators, with a puppet Herodian dynasty and simmering nationalist unrest that would erupt in the Jewish-Roman War of 66-70 AD. Religious life centered on the Temple, Sabbath, and purity law, policed by Pharisees and Sadducees.",
     "Purpose: To prove from the Hebrew Scriptures that Jesus is the promised Messiah and King, organizing His teaching into five major discourses that echo the five books of Moses."),
    ("Mark", "The Suffering Servant in Action",
     "Author & Date: John Mark, companion of both Peter and Paul, likely drawing on Peter's eyewitness preaching; written around the mid-60s AD.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Probably Gentile Christians in Rome, facing the early stages of Neronian hostility toward the church.",
     "Historical Setting: Rome under Nero was increasingly suspicious of Christians, whom it blamed for the fire of Rome in 64 AD. A Gospel showing Jesus as a suffering, serving King who calls His followers to take up a cross would have spoken directly to a persecuted community.",
     "Purpose: The shortest, fastest-moving Gospel (Mark loves the word 'immediately'), it presents Jesus as the Suffering Servant of Isaiah who came 'not to be served, but to serve' (Mark 10:45)."),
    ("Luke", "Savior of the Whole World",
     "Author & Date: Luke, a Gentile physician and traveling companion of Paul, writing a carefully researched, orderly account for a reader named Theophilus (likely a Roman official or new believer of some social standing).",
     "Audience & Occasion: A largely Gentile audience needing assurance that the Christian message was historically reliable and universal in scope.",
     "Historical Setting: The Greco-Roman world of the mid-first century was religiously pluralistic and socially stratified; women, the poor, tax collectors, and Samaritans occupied low social rank. Luke deliberately highlights Jesus' compassion toward exactly these groups.",
     "Purpose: Volume one of a two-part work (Luke-Acts) demonstrating that salvation in Christ is for all people, not only for the privileged or the ritually 'clean.'"),
    ("John", "That You May Believe",
     "Author & Date: The apostle John, writing late in his life, probably from Ephesus in the 80s-90s AD.",
     "Audience & Occasion: A mixed Jewish-Gentile church community starting to face early proto-Gnostic teaching that questioned whether Jesus was truly human.",
     "Historical Setting: Ephesus was a major trade and religious center dominated by the cult of Artemis and a growing marketplace of philosophical and mystical ideas. Against that backdrop, John insists the eternal Word became genuine flesh and blood.",
     "Purpose: Stated explicitly in 20:31 -- 'these are written so that you may continue to believe.' John selects seven public 'signs' and several 'I Am' statements to build a case for Christ's deity and humanity together."),
    ("Acts", "The Gospel Unstoppable",
     "Author & Date: Luke, volume two of Luke-Acts, covering roughly 30 years from the Ascension (c. AD 30-33) to Paul's Roman imprisonment (c. AD 60-62).",
     "Audience & Occasion: Again addressed to Theophilus, tracing how the gospel moved from an upper room in Jerusalem to the capital of the empire.",
     "Historical Setting: The book moves through the real geography and politics of the Roman world -- synagogues, Roman courts, philosophical debate in Athens, trade routes, shipwrecks, and imperial appeals -- giving us an unusually detailed window into first-century Mediterranean life.",
     "Purpose: To show the Holy Spirit empowering ordinary, often persecuted believers to carry the gospel across ethnic and social boundaries, fulfilling Jesus' words in Acts 1:8."),
    ("Romans", "The Gospel Fully Explained",
     "Author & Date: Paul, writing from Corinth around AD 57, preparing the Roman church for a visit and support for a mission further west to Spain.",
     "Audience & Occasion: A church Paul had not founded or visited, made up of both Jewish and Gentile believers navigating real tension over Jewish customs and identity.",
     "Historical Setting: Jewish residents had recently been expelled from Rome under Emperor Claudius (c. AD 49) and were only just returning, creating friction as Gentile believers who had led the church in their absence now had to reintegrate them.",
     "Purpose: Paul's most systematic letter, laying out humanity's universal need for the gospel, justification by faith alone, the role of Israel, and practical unity between Jew and Gentile believers."),
    ("1 Corinthians", "Christ Above Chaos",
     "Author & Date: Paul, writing from Ephesus around AD 55, in response to reports and a letter from the Corinthian church.",
     "Audience & Occasion: A wealthy, cosmopolitan port city church deeply shaped by its surrounding culture of status-seeking, litigation, and sexual permissiveness.",
     "Historical Setting: Corinth sat on a narrow isthmus controlling trade between the Aegean and Ionian seas, hosted the biennial Isthmian Games, and was renowned in the ancient world for wealth, diverse population, and moral looseness -- famously associated with temple prostitution.",
     "Purpose: To correct division, immorality, disorder in worship, and confusion about spiritual gifts and the resurrection, calling the church to Christ-centered unity and love."),
    ("2 Corinthians", "Strength in Weakness",
     "Author & Date: Paul, writing around AD 56, his most emotionally transparent letter, likely from Macedonia.",
     "Audience & Occasion: The same Corinthian church, now facing 'super-apostles' who questioned Paul's authority, appearance, and suffering as marks against his legitimacy.",
     "Historical Setting: Traveling teachers who used polished rhetoric and claimed impressive credentials were common in the Greco-Roman world; Paul's opponents used this cultural expectation to discredit his plain speech and physical hardships.",
     "Purpose: Paul defends his apostleship not by matching their boasting but by highlighting God's power made perfect in weakness, and appeals for generosity toward the suffering Jerusalem church."),
    ("Galatians", "Grace Alone",
     "Author & Date: Paul, likely his earliest letter (mid-to-late 40s AD), written to churches he had planted in the Roman province of Galatia.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Gentile converts being pressured by 'Judaizers' to be circumcised and keep the Mosaic law to be truly saved.",
     "Historical Setting: The tension reflects the wider first-century debate over how much of Jewish law and identity Gentile converts needed to adopt -- a question later formally addressed at the Jerusalem Council (Acts 15).",
     "Purpose: Paul's most urgent, fiery letter, defending justification by faith alone apart from law-keeping, and describing the freedom and fruit that the Spirit produces."),
    ("Ephesians", "The Church, Christ's Body",
     "Author & Date: Paul, written during his Roman imprisonment (c. AD 60-62), likely as a circular letter meant for several churches in the region around Ephesus.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Congregations Paul knew well, needing a vision of the church's identity and unity that transcended their pagan and Jewish backgrounds.",
     "Historical Setting: Ephesus housed the Temple of Artemis, one of the seven wonders of the ancient world, and a thriving trade in magic and the occult (Acts 19:19 describes new believers burning valuable scrolls of sorcery).",
     "Purpose: To describe the cosmic scope of what God has done in Christ, uniting Jew and Gentile into 'one new humanity,' and to call believers to unity, holy living, and spiritual warfare readiness."),
    ("Philippians", "Joy That Doesn't Depend on Circumstances",
     "Author & Date: Paul, written from prison (c. AD 60-62) to the church at Philippi, the first congregation he planted in Europe.",
     "Audience & Occasion: A church that had faithfully partnered with Paul financially since its founding, now sending gifts and concerned about his imprisonment.",
     "Historical Setting: Philippi was a Roman colony populated largely by retired soldiers, intensely proud of its Roman citizenship -- context that sharpens Paul's statement that believers' true 'citizenship is in heaven' (3:20).",
     "Purpose: A warm letter of gratitude and joy, centered on the great Christ-hymn of humility and exaltation in 2:5-11, urging unity and contentment in every circumstance."),
    ("Colossians", "The Supremacy and Sufficiency of Christ",
     "Author & Date: Paul, written from prison around the same time as Ephesians and Philemon, to a church founded not by Paul himself but by his co-worker Epaphras.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Believers being drawn toward an early syncretistic philosophy blending Jewish ritual, ascetic practice, and possibly angel worship or mystical experience.",
     "Historical Setting: Colossae was a smaller city in the Lycus Valley, near Laodicea and Hierapolis, sitting at a crossroads where Greek philosophy, Jewish diaspora communities, and local mystery religions all mixed.",
     "Purpose: To combat that mixture by exalting Christ as fully sufficient -- 'in him the whole fullness of Deity dwells' (2:9) -- so believers need no additional spiritual system."),
    ("1 & 2 Thessalonians", "Hope for the Persecuted and Confused",
     "Author & Date: Paul, among his earliest letters (c. AD 50-51), written shortly after founding the church in Thessalonica during his second missionary journey.",
     "Audience & Occasion: A young, largely Gentile congregation already facing local persecution, and confused about what happens to believers who die before Christ returns.",
     "Historical Setting: Thessalonica was the capital of the Roman province of Macedonia, a busy port on the Via Egnatia, home to an active imperial cult -- proclaiming 'another king, Jesus' (Acts 17:7) was politically provocative there.",
     "Purpose: To comfort grieving believers with the hope of resurrection, correct the false idea that Christ had already returned or that work could be abandoned while waiting, and call the church to holy, orderly living."),
    ("1 & 2 Timothy and Titus", "Guarding the Flock",
     "Author & Date: Paul, near the end of his ministry, writing to two trusted protégés overseeing churches -- Timothy in Ephesus, Titus on the island of Crete.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Young pastors facing false teaching, church disorder, and the practical challenge of raising up qualified leaders.",
     "Historical Setting: Ephesus and Crete both had reputations (the latter proverbially, per the Cretan poet quoted in Titus 1:12) for moral looseness and susceptibility to speculative, myth-laden teaching that had crept into the churches.",
     "Purpose: Deeply practical letters covering doctrine, qualifications for elders and deacons, care for widows, and perseverance in ministry despite opposition and personal hardship."),
    ("Philemon", "The Gospel Reshapes Relationships",
     "Author & Date: Paul, written from prison around the same time as Colossians, to a wealthy believer named Philemon in Colossae.",
     "Audience & Occasion: A personal appeal on behalf of Onesimus, Philemon's runaway slave, who had become a believer and useful helper to Paul.",
     "Historical Setting: Roman law gave slave-owners near-total power over runaway slaves, including the right to severe punishment; Paul does not attack the institution directly but appeals to gospel transformation, urging Philemon to receive Onesimus 'no longer as a slave, but as... a beloved brother' (v. 16).",
     "Purpose: A one-chapter case study in how the gospel quietly subverts unjust social structures from the inside, through changed hearts rather than political revolution."),
    ("Hebrews", "Christ Is Better",
     "Author & Date: Anonymous (early guesses include Paul, Apollos, Barnabas, or Priscilla, with no consensus reached even in the early church), likely written before AD 70 while temple worship still continued.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Jewish Christians under pressure -- possibly considering a retreat back into Judaism and temple sacrifice to avoid persecution.",
     "Historical Setting: For a Jewish believer, walking away from the socially protected status of Judaism to follow the crucified Messiah invited real hostility from family, synagogue, and society; some were tempted to quietly drift back.",
     "Purpose: An extended sermon (literally called a 'word of exhortation,' 13:22) arguing point by point that Christ is superior to angels, Moses, the Levitical priesthood, and the entire old sacrificial system."),
    ("James", "Faith That Works",
     "Author & Date: James, the half-brother of Jesus and leader of the Jerusalem church, writing perhaps as early as the mid-40s AD, making it possibly the earliest New Testament book.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Jewish believers scattered outside Palestine (the 'twelve tribes in the Dispersion,' 1:1), facing trials, economic injustice, and the temptation to a merely intellectual faith.",
     "Historical Setting: Written in the tradition of Hebrew wisdom literature (echoing Proverbs) and closely paralleling the ethical teaching of the Sermon on the Mount, suggesting James had deeply absorbed his brother's teaching even before fully believing in Him (see John 7:5; 1 Corinthians 15:7).",
     "Purpose: A intensely practical letter insisting that genuine faith always produces visible fruit -- in speech, in caring for the poor, and in patient endurance."),
    ("1 & 2 Peter", "Hope Under Pressure",
     "Author & Date: The apostle Peter, writing 1 Peter around AD 62-64 and 2 Peter shortly before his martyrdom under Nero (traditionally c. AD 64-68).",
     "Audience & Occasion: Believers scattered across five Roman provinces in Asia Minor, facing growing social hostility and slander for their faith, and later, false teachers denying Christ's return.",
     "Historical Setting: This was not yet official empire-wide persecution but local social ostracism -- being cut off from trade guilds, family, and civic life for refusing pagan worship -- which is why Peter calls believers 'foreigners and exiles' (1 Peter 2:11).",
     "Purpose: To root suffering believers in the living hope of the resurrection and a coming inheritance, and later to guard the church's doctrine against scoffers and false teachers."),
    ("1, 2 & 3 John", "Walking in the Light of Love and Truth",
     "Author & Date: The apostle John, writing from Ephesus around the same period as his Gospel, late in the first century.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Churches troubled by early teachers (often labeled proto-Gnostic) who denied that Jesus had truly come in human flesh, and who separated 'spiritual' knowledge from moral obedience.",
     "Historical Setting: Greek philosophical dualism, which viewed matter as inferior to spirit, made the idea of God truly becoming flesh seem scandalous to some in the culture -- exactly the claim John insists on (1 John 4:2-3).",
     "Purpose: To give believers confident assurance of salvation through the tests of belief, obedience, and love, and (in the two short personal letters) to guard hospitality toward true teachers and rebuke those who abuse church leadership."),
    ("Jude", "Contend for the Faith",
     "Author & Date: Jude, another half-brother of Jesus and brother of James, writing possibly in the 60s-80s AD.",
     "Audience & Occasion: A church that false teachers had quietly 'slipped in' among (v. 4), teaching that grace excused immoral living.",
     "Historical Setting: Closely parallel in content to 2 Peter 2, Jude draws on both canonical and extra-biblical Jewish tradition (referencing the Book of Enoch and the Assumption of Moses) in ways common to Jewish rhetorical style of the period, without thereby elevating those texts to Scripture.",
     "Purpose: A short, urgent call to 'contend for the faith that was once for all entrusted to God's holy people' (v. 3) against teachers twisting grace into license."),
    ("Revelation", "The Lamb Wins",
     "Author & Date: The apostle John, writing from exile on Patmos, a small Roman penal island in the Aegean, most likely under Emperor Domitian around AD 95.",
     "Audience & Occasion: Seven real churches in the Roman province of Asia, some compromised by comfort and idolatry, others facing real persecution and even martyrdom (2:13).",
     "Historical Setting: Emperor worship was intensifying, and refusing to burn incense to Caesar carried social and sometimes fatal consequences; Revelation's apocalyptic imagery (drawing heavily on Daniel, Ezekiel, and Isaiah) encoded a message of hope that would have been immediately meaningful to its first hearers even where later readers find it puzzling.",
     "Purpose: To unveil the risen, reigning Christ behind the chaos of history, assuring persecuted believers that the Lamb who was slain has already won, and will one day make all things new."),
]

